The main points are:

You need basic ICT skills to work effectively with Linux.
Using Linux regularly helps you learn and build confidence.
Linux administrators don't only manage servers; they may also help users, fix configuration problems, recommend software and update documentation.
Linux distributions can be installed on a computer, often using a USB drive.
The Linux desktop (GUI) is similar to Windows and macOS, with icons, menus and settings.
You can use the GUI to manage things such as user accounts, Wi-Fi and input devices.
Once you are comfortable with the GUI, the next step is learning to use the Linux command line.
 What I have learnt
I have learnt that Linux system administrators need to understand both the Linux desktop (GUI) and the command line. Regularly using Linux helps develop practical skills and confidence. I also learnt that system administrators support users and manage more than just servers.
